# Right-drag: Explorer's ending to a drag — ask which verb was meant instead of inferring one
# from whichever modifier key happened to be down.
#
# The gesture itself is not here and cannot be: a run posts no mouse input, so there is no way to
# hold the right button down and move. What is here is everything the gesture leads to — the menu
# it opens, and the third verb it added.

tree .
refresh

# The ordinary case: all three verbs live, singular wording for one item.
right-drop-menu notes.txt to Documents as 01-one-item

# Plural wording, and nothing else changes.
right-drop-menu notes.txt, report.md to Documents as 02-two-items

# Dropping items into the folder they already live in. Move has nothing to do and is greyed —
# greyed rather than removed, so the row below it does not slide up under the cursor.
right-drop-menu notes.txt to . as 03-same-folder

# Into a container. Adding to an archive rewrites it and is always additive, so there is no move;
# a .lnk stores a path, and an entry inside a container has not got one.
archive-fixture Archives
refresh
right-drop-menu notes.txt to Archives/plain.7z as 04-into-archive

# The verb itself, end to end through the real IShellLink — a file and a folder.
shortcut notes.txt, Documents to Pictures
assert-exists "Pictures/notes.txt - Shortcut.lnk"
assert-exists "Pictures/Documents - Shortcut.lnk"
assert-status Created

# Explorer's "(2)", the same one a paste and a New File produce. Nothing is overwritten.
shortcut notes.txt to Pictures
assert-exists "Pictures/notes.txt - Shortcut (2).lnk"

# Additive, so the undo slot is left pointing at whatever came before rather than spent on links
# the user can select and delete.
assert-cannot-undo

go Pictures
refresh
shot 05-links

state
